# Execution policy is expressed per-action in tools/flutter/defs.bzl, not
# globally: release actions carry `no-sandbox` + `no-remote-exec` so they remain
# remote-cacheable, while debug actions add `local` to opt out entirely.
#
# A global `--spawn_strategy=local` would flatten that distinction and force
# every action local, including ones that could sandbox, so it is deliberately
# not set here.
common --enable_bzlmod

# To share release artifacts across machines, point this at a cache endpoint.
# Note: keys currently embed absolute paths (package_config.json, and the .dill
# it produces), so cross-machine hits will miss until those are canonicalised.
# See the "Remote caching" section of README.md before enabling.
# build --remote_cache=grpc://...

# --- Android -----------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Everything below is correct *for Android* and wrong to impose on a build that
# is not Android. The JDK pin in particular would fix a consumer's entire JVM
# toolchain at 17 to work around a ruleset they may never load. So the flags are
# grouped behind a --config rather than applied unconditionally, and a consumer
# copying this file can take the group without taking the opt-in at the bottom.
#
# This project *is* an Android project, so it opts in on the last line. Remove
# that line and Android builds need an explicit `--config=android`.

# Bazel's manifest merger strips <uses-permission> and <uses-permission-sdk-23>
# from every dependency manifest before merging -- see removePermissions() in
# ManifestMergerAction.java, gated on this flag, which defaults to off. The
# policy behind that default is that an app should declare its own permissions
# rather than inherit them silently.
#
# Flutter plugins are built the other way round: a plugin declares what it needs
# in its own AndroidManifest.xml and Gradle merges it up. connectivity_plus
# declares ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE and nothing else does, so without this flag the
# APK builds, installs, launches and registers the plugin -- and then reports
# the wrong connectivity state at runtime, with no build-time signal at all.
build:android --merge_android_manifest_permissions

# rules_android 0.7.3 builds its own dexer tools (DexFileSplitter et al) from
# source, and they fail to compile under JDK 25: turbine_direct_graal errors out
# running AutoValue/AutoBuilder annotation processors. Pinning the toolchain to
# 17 is what makes android_binary work on Bazel 9.
build:android --tool_java_language_version=17 --tool_java_runtime_version=remotejdk_17
build:android --java_language_version=17 --java_runtime_version=remotejdk_17

# Forward ANDROID_NDK_HOME from the client environment rather than baking a
# path in here, which would be machine-specific. It selects both the NDK
# cc_toolchain (rules_android_ndk) and the CMake toolchain file a native plugin
# is built with, so the two can never disagree.
#
# Must be NDK 28+; //tools/flutter:plugins.bzl refuses older ones. 27 and below
# default to 4 KB page alignment, and such a .so crashes the loader on 16 KB
# devices instead of failing cleanly.
#
# `common:` rather than `build:` because repository rules are evaluated by
# `query` and `mod` too, not only by `build`.
common:android --repo_env=ANDROID_NDK_HOME

# The opt-in. One line, so a consumer building another platform deletes one line
# rather than unpicking four flags from their comments.
common --config=android
